  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "Transfer operation is not atomic: the source balance check, debit, and credit are separate queries without a transaction. Concurrent transfers can overspend/overdraw source accounts, and a failure between debit and credit can permanently lose funds.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "No existence check for the destination account before crediting; a transfer to a nonexistent account ID may silently succeed from the caller's perspective while the credit has no effect.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "description": "No validation that `amount` is positive and finite. A negative amount would reverse the transfer direction and bypass the intended funds-availability check.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "HOLD",
  "fix_description": "Implement the transfer as a single atomic operation wrapped in a database transaction. Use SELECT ... FOR UPDATE when reading the source row (and verify the source exists), check that the destination account exists before crediting, validate that amount is a finite positive number (reject amount <= 0 or non-finite values), then debit the source and credit the destination within the same transaction. Commit only after both updates succeed; roll back on any failure so concurrent transfers cannot overdraw and no funds are lost between debit and credit. Return appropriate errors for insufficient funds, missing accounts, or invalid amounts.",
